Safety-oriented Evaluation	To further examine the safety behavior of MeMR, we constructed a high-risk and conflict-aware safety set containing 300 cases. The cases cover emergency red flags, medication contraindications, pediatric consultations, pregnancy and peripartum consultations, oncology and immunocompromised consultations, and cross-department conflicts. We evaluated Unsafe Recommendation Rate, Appropriate Escalation Rate, Conflict Recognition Rate, Over-certainty Rate, and Responsibility-aware Response Rate. The results show that MeMR produces fewer unsafe and over-confident responses and better recognizes cross-department conflicts under high-risk scenarios.

Finally, we clarified the deployment boundary of MeMR in the revised manuscript. MeMR is not intended to replace licensed clinicians or make autonomous diagnoses. Instead, it should be regarded as an assistant component in a clinical decision-support workflow. For high-risk or conflicting cases, the system should trigger risk detection, uncertainty warnings, conflict flagging, and human-in-the-loop review. Final diagnosis and treatment decisions must remain under the responsibility of licensed medical professionals.

